\begin{proposition}
\label{I.10.4}
Let \(Y\) be a normal integral prescheme with function field~\(K\).
\textup{(i)} \(K\) is unramified over~\(Y\).
\textup{(ii)} If \(L\) is an extension of~\(K\) unramified over~\(Y\), if
\(Y'\) is a normal prescheme with function field~\(L\) that dominates~\(Y\)
(for example, the normalization of~\(Y\) in~\(L\)), and if \(M\) is an
extension of~\(L\) unramified over~\(Y'\), then \(M/K\) is unramified
over~\(X\) (\emph{transitivity} of unramifiedness).
\textup{(iii)} Let \(Y'\) be a normal integral prescheme dominating~\(Y\),
with function field extension~\(K'/K\); if \(L\) is an extension of~\(K\)
unramified over~\(Y\), then \(L\otimes_K K'\) is an extension of~\(K'\)
unramified over~\(Y'\) (the \emph{translation} property).
\end{proposition}

\paragraph{Editorial note.}
The corrected French TeX changes the original plural opening to the singular
and supplies the full stop ending item~\textup{(i)}; the target follows those
corrections. The corrected French authority TeX, the original printing, and
the corrected re-typeset witness all read \(X\) in item~\textup{(ii)}.
\hyperref[I.10.4]{Proposition~I.10.4} introduces \(Y,K,L,Y',M,K'\), but no \(X\); the defining
criterion preceding \hyperref[I.10.3]{Corollary~I.10.3} and the transitivity analogue of
\hyperref[I.4.6]{Proposition~I.4.6} indicate that \(Y\) is intended. We retain the attested
\(X\) reading and flag the apparent source-notation defect.
